<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Email's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because most sections
    that do anything for a human tell them about it by mail: they build a message through
    IEmailMessageFactory and send it through IEmailService. A section may not reference
    another section's implementation project, so putting these types in a Contracts/
    *folder* inside Humans.Email would leave every one of those callers with no way to name
    them (memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md — split the upstream section's
    contracts downward, never promote the shared type into Base). Shell consumes the leaf
    too, composing the HangfireImmediateOutboxProcessor -> IImmediateOutboxProcessor ->
    OutboxEmailService chain in EmailInfrastructureExtensions.

    Base is *not* among the consumers: its single ProjectReference is Humans.Users.Contracts
    and it names no type from this project.

    IEmailOutboxProcessor / IEmailOutboxRetention no longer earn their place here: their
    only consumers, ProcessEmailOutboxJob and CleanupEmailOutboxJob, are now section-side.
    Both could move inward; IImmediateOutboxProcessor could not, because Shell registers it.
    See Docs/health.md §5.

    Everything with no consumer outside the section stays internal: the outbox admin
    surface (IEmailOutboxService), the renderer, the body composer, the SMTP transport,
    the repository and the entity.

    References Humans.Base (IApplicationService, and EmailOutboxStatus, which lives there
    because Campaigns and Surveys persist it too), Humans.Events.Contracts (EventStatus,
    named by EventLifecycleNotification), and Humans.Users.Contracts, for MembershipTier
    and MessageCategory — both Users' own vocabulary, named here by IEmailMessageFactory's
    audience parameters and EmailMessage's Category. That is the enums sitting with their
    owner, not Email taking a dependency on Users' services.

    Both leaf-to-leaf edges are acyclic. Humans.Events.Contracts has zero
    ProjectReference; Humans.Users.Contracts has exactly one, to the equally
    zero-reference Humans.Onboarding.Contracts. Both chains terminate. Note that Base
    references Humans.Users.Contracts, so nothing in that project may ever reference this one.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Base\Humans.Base.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Events.Contracts\Humans.Events.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Users.Contracts\Humans.Users.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
